\title{Operations on modular equivalences}
\date{2026-04-10T13:54:09Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\taxon{Remark}
\p{Note that when we have an \em{equality} #{a = b}, we can do whatever we want to it: as long as we do the same thing to both sides they will remain equal. With #{\equiv_m}, however, this only works for certain operations! The things we do to an equivalence have to “play nice” with the operation of taking remainders modulo #{m}.}
\p{The [previous theorem](00E2) shows that we can add, subtract, or multiply both sides of a modular equivalence by the same thing.  However, it turns out that we \em{cannot} divide—at least, not in a naïve way!  For example, notice that ##{2 \equiv_4 6,} but if we try dividing both sides by 2, we get ##{1 \equiv_4 3,} which is false!}




